1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,140
Hello everyone and welcome back to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:05,860
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:10,140
My name is Catherine and today we have a very special lesson for everyone.

4
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:11,800
It's a new level for us.

5
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:12,440
Right.

6
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:16,780
It's an advanced media lesson and we are going to be listening to an apology.

7
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:21,360
Well, it's an apology letter but we're going to actually have someone talk it out.

8
00:00:21,820 --> 00:00:22,040
Right.

9
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:39,880
So, what's happening here is someone is reading a letter they've written and they're trying to apologize for something and we don't really know what this something is but I want everyone to really listen here to the words that are being used and to the ways in which we can say sorry because as they say, there are a thousand ways to say I'm sorry.

10
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:52,280
Dear Mary, I come here today this way because I need to apologize to you.

11
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:53,660
I failed you.

12
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:58,980
Although I did not lie to you in words, I lied to you with faces that did not belong to me.

13
00:00:59,740 --> 00:01:02,940
I never meant to ruin the friendship that meant the world to me.

14
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:07,080
You mean the world to me and now I come to you asking for forgiveness.

15
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:12,560
If in your heart you find you can't, then I will understand and learn from this experience.

16
00:01:13,990 --> 00:01:16,830
You came into my life at a time when I needed you the most.

17
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:24,880
We talked about so many things that I started to realize my heart and my soul could actually feel something other than hurt.

18
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:33,200
You placed comfort where there was fear, confidence when there was doubt, a shoulder where tears could fall and completeness where there was emptiness.

19
00:01:34,440 --> 00:01:38,370
I wanted to hold on to this so badly that I did whatever it took for you to notice.

20
00:01:39,110 --> 00:01:47,000
What I didn't realize was that I could lose my entire being, all of who I was and all of that I had placed in you.

21
00:01:47,950 --> 00:01:50,830
I wanted to be the one who would be there when you needed to talk.

22
00:01:51,610 --> 00:01:55,230
I wanted to be the comfort for your soul when the world was too much to handle.

23
00:01:56,030 --> 00:01:59,090
I wanted to be strong for you when everything else seemed impossible.

24
00:01:59,740 --> 00:02:07,260
I wanted to love you in only the way you deserve to be loved, never realizing that I was destroying myself and you.

25
00:02:08,170 --> 00:02:10,950
Somehow I needed you to be a part of my life.

26
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:15,480
The only problem was that I was willing to jeopardize everything to get that done.

27
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:19,960
All the things that I told you about how I felt and how you make me feel were true.

28
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:24,540
Nothing else mattered to me except hearing the laughter in your voice when you were happy.

29
00:02:25,180 --> 00:02:27,860
You made my days easy to get through and my nights peaceful.

30
00:02:28,490 --> 00:02:30,390
You helped me look forward to another day.

31
00:02:31,360 --> 00:02:34,500
Even though distance separated us, just being was enough.

32
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:42,520
I'm sorry for hurting you and if I had to do it all over again, I would have been 100% with you.

33
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:43,110
Forgive me please.

34
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:51,600
All right, we're back.

35
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:53,380
So the guy seemed to be pretty sad.

36
00:02:53,980 --> 00:02:55,800
Yeah, this is a very intense.

37
00:02:56,100 --> 00:02:57,360
Well, it's not a dialogue.

38
00:02:57,560 --> 00:02:58,240
It's a monologue.

39
00:02:58,640 --> 00:02:59,440
One person is reading it.

40
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:01,340
So it's a very intense monologue.

41
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:02,300
Very romantic.

42
00:03:03,100 --> 00:03:05,620
Yeah, romantic, but also an air of melancholy.

43
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:07,200
Yeah, that's true.

44
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:14,080
He's kind of like apologizing and at the same time expressing love and all these feelings.

45
00:03:14,100 --> 00:03:15,340
So it was very interesting.

46
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:18,380
But why don't we take a look at a couple of phrases.

47
00:03:18,820 --> 00:03:29,680
Now in advanced media, we're not going to analyze the entire dialogue or as we do in the other levels, actually pick out items for language takeaway or fluency builder.

48
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:36,100
So in advanced media, we're only going to pick out a couple of words and phrases and just talk about it and talk about the topic in general.

49
00:03:36,630 --> 00:03:37,150
Exactly.

50
00:03:37,450 --> 00:03:45,410
So one of the first phrases that we decided to talk about here is this phrase, I failed you because this is really the theme of the letter.

51
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:46,840
Right.

52
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:49,670
So if you fail someone, it means that you let that person down.

53
00:03:50,130 --> 00:03:55,130
Okay, so you fail someone, I failed you, or you can say, he failed me.

54
00:03:55,630 --> 00:04:02,310
So when I really needed that person the most, that person didn't come through or let me down or failed me.

55
00:04:02,850 --> 00:04:04,250
All right, so let's give an example maybe.

56
00:04:05,360 --> 00:04:08,230
I needed my brother to pick me up because I don't have a car.

57
00:04:09,040 --> 00:04:11,990
And my brother was supposed to pick me up at four o'clock and he forgot.

58
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:12,850
He didn't come.

59
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:14,590
He really failed me.

60
00:04:14,840 --> 00:04:15,950
All right, he failed you.

61
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:16,790
Yeah, I was disappointed.

62
00:04:17,230 --> 00:04:21,830
Well, this isn't that's not as serious as what happened here in this letter, I think.

63
00:04:22,240 --> 00:04:23,240
Right?

64
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:24,240
I think so.

65
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:25,240
But we could talk about what did happen.

66
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:26,240
I think I have a theory.

67
00:04:26,240 --> 00:04:27,240
And I know you have a theory.

68
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:28,320
So we can talk about that in a minute.

69
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:32,660
But before that, we have some other phrases about forgiveness and disappointment.

70
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:33,880
Right.

71
00:04:33,880 --> 00:04:38,840
So he also said, you mean the world to me and now I come to you asking for forgiveness.

72
00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:40,920
Okay, so we've got a positive and a negative.

73
00:04:41,160 --> 00:04:44,040
So the positive here is you mean the world to me.

74
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:45,800
You mean the world to me.

75
00:04:45,920 --> 00:04:49,210
This means you are so important to me.

76
00:04:49,330 --> 00:04:51,390
You're one of the most important people in my life.

77
00:04:51,730 --> 00:04:54,650
You're worth the entire world, in my opinion.

78
00:04:55,010 --> 00:04:57,250
Yeah, it's a very poetic way of saying you're very important.

79
00:04:58,560 --> 00:05:01,210
And then he says, and now I come asking for your forgiveness.

80
00:05:01,890 --> 00:05:02,150
Okay.

81
00:05:02,290 --> 00:05:05,490
So this implies that he did something wrong.

82
00:05:06,150 --> 00:05:07,810
I come asking for forgiveness.

83
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:10,240
So in English, we ask for forgiveness.

84
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:12,240
You can also beg for forgiveness.

85
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:14,760
So please, please forgive me.

86
00:05:14,760 --> 00:05:16,920
But you can't demand forgiveness.

87
00:05:16,920 --> 00:05:18,280
I demand you forgive.

88
00:05:18,280 --> 00:05:19,880
No, that doesn't quite work.

89
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:20,030
All right.

90
00:05:20,880 --> 00:05:21,880
What else do we have?

91
00:05:21,880 --> 00:05:22,670
We've got the world again here.

92
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:28,560
He says he, the guy who's reading, he says, you know, I wanted to be the comfort for your soul.

93
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:32,670
So this is very, again, poetic, when the world was too much to handle.

94
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:36,040
Too much to handle is a really important English phrase.

95
00:05:36,060 --> 00:05:38,360
And I'm sure you've heard it before, but we could talk about a little bit here.

96
00:05:38,560 --> 00:05:38,680
Right.

97
00:05:38,700 --> 00:05:41,780
So he says when everything else was too much to handle.

98
00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:44,800
So everything was so difficult or it was overwhelming.

99
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:45,800
Right.

100
00:05:46,200 --> 00:05:50,940
So you could say even in the office, man, all this work I have to do, it's too much to handle.

101
00:05:51,180 --> 00:05:51,720
Right, right.

102
00:05:52,440 --> 00:05:53,440
Okay.

103
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:54,440
Too much to handle.

104
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:55,760
You could also say a child is too much to handle.

105
00:05:56,240 --> 00:05:57,420
I think they said this about my brother.

106
00:05:57,680 --> 00:05:59,120
He was very difficult growing up.

107
00:05:59,120 --> 00:05:59,440
Too hard.

108
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:02,120
He's too, he's too difficult to handle.

109
00:06:02,230 --> 00:06:02,770
All right.

110
00:06:03,120 --> 00:06:10,300
Well, what about in the part where he says you place comfort where there was fear, confidence where there was doubt.

111
00:06:10,300 --> 00:06:15,670
So this structure is interesting because you placed something where there used to be something else.

112
00:06:16,640 --> 00:06:19,650
So you're replacing something, you're changing something for something else.

113
00:06:20,440 --> 00:06:20,650
It's an exchange.

114
00:06:21,440 --> 00:06:26,360
So you could say you placed comfort where there was fear, you placed happiness, where there was sadness.

115
00:06:26,360 --> 00:06:27,360
Where there was sadness.

116
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:28,930
So it's always like kind of opposites, right?

117
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:30,400
Exactly.

118
00:06:30,400 --> 00:06:31,400
Okay.

119
00:06:31,400 --> 00:06:32,400
Light where there was dark.

120
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:33,400
Right, exactly.

121
00:06:33,400 --> 00:06:35,600
So that's an interesting format of saying something.

122
00:06:35,600 --> 00:06:37,860
You did something where there used to be something else.

123
00:06:38,440 --> 00:06:39,600
All right.

124
00:06:39,600 --> 00:06:42,080
And what about this word to jeopardize?

125
00:06:42,080 --> 00:06:43,080
All right.

126
00:06:43,180 --> 00:06:43,660
This is a good one.

127
00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:48,000
I always think of the TV game show Jeopardy, but that's a noun.

128
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:55,520
This here is a verb to jeopardize and that means to put something in danger or to risk losing something.

129
00:06:55,520 --> 00:06:55,820
Okay.

130
00:06:56,120 --> 00:06:59,060
So to jeopardize your health if you're doing something dangerous.

131
00:06:59,240 --> 00:07:02,200
So smoking jeopardizes one's health.

132
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:03,280
You're putting your health at risk.

133
00:07:04,080 --> 00:07:09,600
Or I don't want to jeopardize my savings by investing everything I have in stocks.

134
00:07:10,560 --> 00:07:13,000
That means I would prefer to do something safer.

135
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:17,600
So you don't like to jeopardize your well-being.

136
00:07:17,660 --> 00:07:19,520
I like to play it safe, as we say.

137
00:07:20,080 --> 00:07:20,260
All right.

138
00:07:21,080 --> 00:07:22,080
Very good.

139
00:07:22,080 --> 00:07:30,560
And last but not least, this almost the last phrase when he says, even though distance separated us, just being was enough.

140
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:32,860
What does he mean when he says just being was enough?

141
00:07:33,100 --> 00:07:33,280
All right.

142
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:35,180
This is very romantic, intensely romantic.

143
00:07:35,440 --> 00:07:43,840
He says, even though we were far apart, just being, so just existing, just breathing was enough.

144
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:47,440
So just knowing that she's in the world was enough for him to feel happy.

145
00:07:47,920 --> 00:07:50,100
So that's what he means with just being was enough.

146
00:07:50,440 --> 00:07:50,660
Right.

147
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:52,550
So just being is existing.

148
00:07:53,600 --> 00:07:54,230
It's being alive.

149
00:07:54,950 --> 00:07:55,410
Okay.

150
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:56,170
Very good.

151
00:07:56,630 --> 00:08:00,430
I think we should listen to this apology letter one more time and we'll be back.

152
00:08:07,790 --> 00:08:12,970
Dear Mary, I come here today this way because I need to apologize to you.

153
00:08:13,450 --> 00:08:14,090
I failed you.

154
00:08:14,930 --> 00:08:19,650
Although I did not lie to you in words, I lied to you with faces that did not belong to me.

155
00:08:20,650 --> 00:08:23,510
I never meant to ruin the friendship that meant the world to me.

156
00:08:24,010 --> 00:08:25,350
You mean the world to me.

157
00:08:25,650 --> 00:08:27,870
And now I come to you asking for forgiveness.

158
00:08:28,810 --> 00:08:33,390
If in your heart you find you can't, then I will understand and learn from this experience.

159
00:08:34,870 --> 00:08:37,670
You came into my life at a time when I needed you the most.

160
00:08:38,560 --> 00:08:45,700
We talked about so many things that I started to realize my heart and my soul could actually feel something other than hurt.

161
00:08:45,700 --> 00:08:54,050
You placed comfort where there was fear, confidence when there was doubt, a shoulder where tears could fall and completeness where there was emptiness.

162
00:08:55,240 --> 00:08:59,190
I wanted to hold on to this so badly that I did whatever it took for you to notice.

163
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:07,810
What I didn't realize was that I could lose my entire being, all of who I was and all of that I had placed in you.

164
00:09:08,820 --> 00:09:11,640
I wanted to be the one who would be there when you needed to talk.

165
00:09:12,460 --> 00:09:16,040
I wanted to be the comfort for your soul when the world was too much to handle.

166
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,880
I wanted to be strong for you when everything else seemed impossible.

167
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:28,070
I wanted to love you in only the way you deserve to be loved, never realizing that I was destroying myself and you.

168
00:09:29,120 --> 00:09:31,780
Somehow I needed you to be a part of my life.

169
00:09:32,520 --> 00:09:36,300
The only problem was that I was willing to jeopardize everything to get that done.

170
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:40,760
All the things that I told you about how I felt and how you make me feel were true.

171
00:09:41,440 --> 00:09:45,340
Nothing else mattered to me except hearing the laughter in your voice when you were happy.

172
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:48,660
You made my days easy to get through and my nights peaceful.

173
00:09:49,500 --> 00:09:51,200
You helped me look forward to another day.

174
00:09:51,960 --> 00:09:55,300
Even though distance separated us, just being was enough.

175
00:09:56,600 --> 00:10:03,360
I'm sorry for hurting you and if I had to do it all over again, I would have been 100% with you.

176
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:12,800
Forgive me please.

177
00:10:12,800 --> 00:10:15,770
Alright so what do you think he did that he's apologizing in this way?

178
00:10:16,120 --> 00:10:17,570
Because I don't think there were a couple right?

179
00:10:17,880 --> 00:10:20,250
Because he mentions friendship a couple of times.

180
00:10:21,280 --> 00:10:22,280
Yes with a but.

181
00:10:22,280 --> 00:10:25,250
You don't write a letter like this to someone who you want to stay friends with.

182
00:10:25,480 --> 00:10:31,080
You write a letter to this to someone who you have some kind of romantic or intense feeling for right?

183
00:10:31,080 --> 00:10:39,720
So to me this seems like a letter that was written to someone who was a great friend and he's afraid of losing that because he cares even more for her.

184
00:10:39,720 --> 00:10:40,720
Right.

185
00:10:40,720 --> 00:10:44,920
But yeah I guess but do you think he's confessing his love in a certain way here?

186
00:10:45,680 --> 00:10:47,720
I think in some ways he's confessing a love.

187
00:10:48,320 --> 00:10:49,600
You can love your friends that's okay.

188
00:10:50,380 --> 00:10:55,740
But more than that he's just he's apologizing for something that he thinks has completely destroyed their friendship.

189
00:10:56,520 --> 00:10:59,260
And that is where I'm very confused because I have no idea what he did.

190
00:11:00,570 --> 00:11:05,770
Well maybe he did something that let her down.

191
00:11:06,030 --> 00:11:09,050
Maybe he became jealous of her new boyfriend or something like that.

192
00:11:09,270 --> 00:11:18,530
Yeah or maybe it's something catty like he was supposed to do something with her and ended up doing something with other people and left her out or embarrassed her in front of people.

193
00:11:19,090 --> 00:11:19,910
It's hard to say.

194
00:11:20,120 --> 00:11:22,040
Now have you ever written an apology letter?

195
00:11:22,040 --> 00:11:23,960
Yeah I have.

196
00:11:23,960 --> 00:11:25,370
I've written a few apology letters.

197
00:11:25,960 --> 00:11:27,470
I have a very strong sense of guilt.

198
00:11:28,210 --> 00:11:29,450
I was raised Catholic.

199
00:11:29,880 --> 00:11:32,200
So an apology letter.

200
00:11:32,200 --> 00:11:38,030
Is it do you usually plan out what you want to say or you just like you just splurge your feelings onto the paper?

201
00:11:38,560 --> 00:11:39,050
Well you know me.

202
00:11:39,560 --> 00:11:44,120
My personality is very planned and organized and so normally I have points.

203
00:11:44,440 --> 00:11:47,160
So I say if this is the problem there's three parts.

204
00:11:47,240 --> 00:11:50,600
I have an answer for each part and the very end I have the heartfelt apology.

205
00:11:50,600 --> 00:11:51,600
Wow.

206
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:52,020
It's like an essay.

207
00:11:52,600 --> 00:11:54,560
I guess it's a little bit more organized.

208
00:11:54,560 --> 00:11:58,480
If I were to write something like this I would just like start writing and whatever comes out.

209
00:11:59,160 --> 00:12:01,400
That would probably make less sense than your letter.

210
00:12:01,400 --> 00:12:09,150
Well I just try to stay on focus, stay on point and address the matter at hand because otherwise then you get into other stuff and you can make people more angry.

211
00:12:09,680 --> 00:12:10,680
Right.

212
00:12:10,680 --> 00:12:12,210
But it is really important to write an apology letter.

213
00:12:12,450 --> 00:12:19,370
Now some of the words and phrases and the vocabulary used here can be used in circumstances that are not only romantic.

214
00:12:19,960 --> 00:12:23,880
You can use it in a professional way as well.

215
00:12:23,880 --> 00:12:24,090
That's true.

216
00:12:24,880 --> 00:12:27,190
In a professional sense you want to be careful not to get too personal.

217
00:12:27,630 --> 00:12:36,370
So in a professional sense it's good to say that you're sorry for any mix ups or for misunderstandings but in the future you will try to make things better or improve.

218
00:12:36,920 --> 00:12:48,640
But in friendships and in families and in romantic relationships apology letters are very very important because I'm a big believer in mending bridges.

219
00:12:48,640 --> 00:12:55,400
I know a lot of people who we say in English to burn a bridge that means to destroy a relationship and never go back.

220
00:12:55,400 --> 00:13:02,520
But I like to mend bridges to make sure that people who I was friends with don't, you know if we have a fight don't just hate me forever.

221
00:13:02,520 --> 00:13:06,760
That even if there's a disagreement that they understand my perspective or that I understand theirs.

222
00:13:06,760 --> 00:13:07,760
That's true.

223
00:13:07,760 --> 00:13:08,760
I think that's important.

224
00:13:08,760 --> 00:13:09,760
What about apology emails?

225
00:13:09,760 --> 00:13:10,760
Do you agree with those?

226
00:13:10,760 --> 00:13:13,470
I think that they are a necessity of the 21st century.

227
00:13:14,370 --> 00:13:18,890
I mean I live far away from most of my friends and family and so it's important for me to be able to communicate in any way.

228
00:13:19,560 --> 00:13:28,640
But and sometimes I mean I'm very shy with some people so sometimes it's hard for me to talk on the phone because I get stuck and then I don't I can't think of what I wanted to say.

229
00:13:28,640 --> 00:13:37,400
And so if a letter helps in that way to get you past your fear or your anxiety then by all means an email is just as much.

230
00:13:37,640 --> 00:13:41,540
I actually received a letter, a handwritten letter through an email.

231
00:13:41,940 --> 00:13:42,740
How did that happen?

232
00:13:42,880 --> 00:13:45,340
So the person, they scanned it, they scanned the letter.

233
00:13:45,600 --> 00:13:45,940
Oh wow.

234
00:13:46,600 --> 00:13:49,460
So it was still like it still had a very intimate and personal sense.

235
00:13:49,920 --> 00:13:54,400
But obviously since the mail takes so long and everything they just scanned it and then they sent it to my email.

236
00:13:54,640 --> 00:13:54,880
That's nice.

237
00:13:55,060 --> 00:13:56,160
So I think that's a good idea.

238
00:13:56,440 --> 00:13:57,440
Maybe you could try that one.

239
00:13:57,800 --> 00:13:58,160
I'll try that one.

240
00:13:58,630 --> 00:14:00,870
I don't agree with the one line email.

241
00:14:01,270 --> 00:14:02,150
I'm sorry, forgive me.

242
00:14:02,440 --> 00:14:03,800
Yeah, yeah, that's not good.

243
00:14:03,800 --> 00:14:11,550
As my parents always used to say when I was growing up and I'd get in fights with my little brother, the only apologies that we accept are the ones that are from the heart.

244
00:14:11,950 --> 00:14:17,230
So I used to have to apologize again and again and again until I actually meant it.

245
00:14:17,320 --> 00:14:31,560
And so I think that this is a very true kind of idea that, well maybe not in business and work where you have to be cordial, but with friends and real relationships with people who you care about, obviously real apologies are very important.

246
00:14:31,560 --> 00:14:31,780
Of course.

